== Formal Semantics of Pattern Matching <sec:case-semantics>

The semantics of all pattern matching constructs other than `case`
expressions are defined by giving identities that relate those
constructs to `case` expressions.  The semantics of `case` expressions
themselves are in turn given as a series of identities, in
@fig:case1, @fig:case2 and @fig:case3.

In these rules, $e$, $e'$ and $e_i$ are expressions; $g_i$ and $italic("gs")_i$
are guards and sequences of guards; $p$ and $p_i$ are patterns; $v$, $x$
and $x_i$ are variables; $y$ is a fresh variable; $K$ and $K'$ are
algebraic datatype (`data`) constructors (including tuple constructors);
and $N$ is a `newtype` constructor.

The report writes rules (b), (c), (g) and (s) with ellipses that run
through nested `case` expressions.  Ott cannot express these, so they
are restated as one-step identities that unfold to the same nested
form: (b) and (c) peel off one alternative or one guarded right-hand side
at a time, and (g) uses pattern guards, which (s) and (t) turn into the
report's nested `case` expressions, left to right.

