= Syntax Reference <ch:syntax>

== Layout

The layout algorithm $L$ adds braces and semicolons to a stream of
lexemes, in which $\{n\}$ marks the start of a layout block after `let`,
`where`, `do` or `of`, and $<n>$ the indentation of the first lexeme of a
line, given a stack of layout contexts: 0 for an explicit context, or
the indentation of an implicit one.  The application $L space
italic("tokens") space []$ gives a layout-insensitive translation of
$italic("tokens")$.

In the definition below, "`:`" is stream construction, "`[]`" the empty
stream, and $t$ a lexeme other than a brace.  The report's equations are
tried in order, the later ones applying only when the earlier ones do
not; here each states the conditions under which it applies, so exactly
one applies to a given stream and stack of contexts, except at the end
of the input in an explicit context, where none does.

#let row(rule, note: none) = {
  let (lhs, eq, rhs, cond) = rule(cells: true)
  let n = if note != none { [(Note #note)] }
  (lhs, eq, rhs, [#cond #n])
}
#block[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, 1fr),
    align: (left, center, left, left),
    stroke: none,
    ..row(layout.ottdruleindentXXsemi),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleindentXXclose),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleindentXXskip),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleindentXXskipXXtop),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleblockXXopen, note: 1),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleblockXXopenXXtop, note: 1),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleblockXXempty, note: 2),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleblockXXemptyXXtop, note: 2),
    ..row(layout.ottdrulecloseXXexplicit, note: 3),
    ..row(layout.ottdrulecloseXXerror, note: 3),
    ..row(layout.ottdrulecloseXXerrorXXtop, note: 3),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleopenXXexplicit, note: 4),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleparseXXerror, note: 5),
    ..row(layout.ottdruletoken),
    ..row(layout.ottdruletokenXXtop),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleend),
    ..row(layout.ottdruleendXXclose, note: 6),
  )
]

The notes are those of the report.  Note 1 says that a nested context
must be further indented than the enclosing one, and that otherwise $L$
fails; Note 2 that a block not indented further is empty; Note 3 that an
explicit close brace only matches an explicit open brace; Note 4 that all
brace pairs are explicit layout contexts; Note 5 that
$italic("parse-error")(t)$ holds if the tokens generated so far followed
by $t$ are an invalid prefix of the Haskell grammar, and followed by "`}`"
a valid one; and Note 6 that pending close braces are inserted at the end
of the input, where an explicit context is an error.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Observations]

Stating the conditions of each equation shows that the equation of
Note 2 applies in exactly the case where Note 1 says that $L$ fails
($n <= m$ for $\{n\}$): $L$ inserts empty braces instead, and the error
only shows up later as a parse error.  The report also says, after the
notes, that "Note 1 implements the feature that layout processing can be
stopped prematurely by a parse error"; that is Note 5.

#heading(numbering: none)[What is not formalised]

The document covers every "Translation" box of the report, the formal
semantics of `case` expressions, the identities of the syntax of types,
and the layout algorithm.  The rest of the report is not formalised:

- The lexical structure (Chapter 2) and the context-free grammar
  (Chapter 10) are not transcribed: the abstract syntax here collapses the
  precedence levels of expressions into one nonterminal, with Ott parsing
  priorities in their place, and covers only the declarations that the
  translations need (bindings, type signatures, and `data` declarations
  with strictness flags).  Classes, instances, `newtype`, `type`, record
  fields in `data`, and modules (Chapter 5) are not covered.
- Fixity resolution (Chapter 10) is given in the report as Haskell code,
  which is better checked by running it than by restating it.
- The static semantics, which the report gives in prose (type classes,
  kinds, defaulting, the monomorphism restriction), would be a much larger
  project.
- The Prelude and libraries (Chapters 6, 7 and 9), the foreign function
  interface (Chapter 8), derived instances (Chapter 11) and pragmas
  (Chapter 12) are given as code or prose and have no translations.
